  "knowledgePattern": "Track a two-term recurrence modulo four and count zero states",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "sequences"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Only remainders modulo 4 matter. Starting from 1,1, the first remainders are 1,1,2,3,1,0,1,1. The ordered pair (1,1) has returned.</p><p>Because each next remainder is the sum of the previous two modulo 4, that ordered pair determines every later remainder. The six-term block 1,1,2,3,1,0 therefore repeats.</p><p>A term is divisible by 4 precisely at an index divisible by 6. Among the first 100 indices these are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>96</mn></mrow></math>, giving 16 terms. Answer B.</p>",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use remainders instead of large numbers",
      "body": "<p>Start with remainders 1 and 1. Add the last two as usual, but replace each answer by its remainder after division by 4.</p>",
      "recap": "A term is divisible by 4 exactly when its remainder is zero.",
      "pitfall": "Keep remainders between 0 and 3.",
      "pause": "Make a table with the term number and its remainder.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l03",
          "reminder": "A term is divisible by 4 exactly when its remainder is zero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Look for the starting pair again",
      "body": "<p>Continue until two consecutive remainders are 1, 1 again. Since each new term uses the previous two, the same pair starts the same pattern again.</p>",
      "recap": "A two-term rule needs a repeated pair, not just one repeated value.",
      "pitfall": "Keep the term numbers aligned when the pattern restarts.",
      "pause": "Mark the zero remainders, then count their positions among the first 100 terms.",
